      "Language": "ita",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Ergonovine testing was carried out in a selected group of 25 patients with Prinzmetal's variant angina treated with calcium-antagonists in order to: define its usefulness in the evaluation of the short-term effectiveness of calcium-antagonist treatment; compare the results of the test with those of Holter monitoring; verify if the results of the test during the acute phase are correlated with the long-term response to treatment. In all patients a control period lasting 2-6 days was carried out, after which a treatment period with calcium-antagonists (nifedipine, diltiazem, verapamil), lasting 2-8 days, was instituted. In 20 patients only 1 calcium-antagonist was evaluated, in 1 patient 2 calcium-antagonists and in 4 all of them. Scalar ergonovine test was carried out in control conditions and repeated during each calcium-antagonist treatment period. During both control and treatment periods all patients underwent Holter monitoring for evaluation of frequency of the spontaneous attacks. After the acute phase 21 of the 25 patients were discharged on calcium-antagonist treatment and followed-up for a mean period of 11 +/- 7 months. In control conditions ergonovine test was positive in 24 patients at a mean dose of 0.11 +/- 0.09 mg.(ABSTRACT TRUNCATED AT 250 WORDS)"},
